  arena flips caused by that fp32 band: Q 2/32768 halves, K 0/262144 halves; the largest pattern delta is 1 fp16 ulp, and 0 flips land on a binade edge where the fp16 exponent field itself changes (that is legal rounding: 0.9995 becomes 1.000)
    t=4  h=5  dim=36  mirror -0.101532 vs card -0.101532: gap 5.960e-08 = 0.001 of one fp16 cell; tie distance 0.0010 (side +1) vs 0.0000 (side +0) -> half AE7F becomes AE80 = 1 fp16 ulp
    t=1  h=11 dim=185 mirror 0.851807 vs card 0.851807: gap 1.192e-07 = 0.000 of one fp16 cell; tie distance 0.0002 (side +1) vs 0.0000 (side +0) -> half 3AD1 becomes 3AD0 = 1 fp16 ulp
  verdict: of the 2 flips, 0 have the two fp32 values on strictly OPPOSITE sides of the cell boundary and 2 have one side sitting EXACTLY on it (a true RNE tie, decided by the even-mantissa rule). No flip disagrees by more than 1 fp16 ulp for a reason that is not rounding: the ABSOLUTE disagreement peaks at 1.192e-07, inside leg R's own fp32 band of 1.192e-06, and the widest ulp counts belong to dims where the rotation nearly cancelled (small value, small cell). 2 of 294912 halves = 0.0007%.
  the angle at pair p is position * 1e7^(-p/32): p0=1 p1=0.604296 p2=0.365174 p3=0.220673 and by p8 it is 1.8e-02, i.e. cos=1.000000 and sin=angle exactly in fp32 for every position we run. So a mismatch at large p is an algebra bug, and one confined to small p is the transcendental gap of leg T.

